QUIZ MANAGEMENT SYSTEM USING C PROGRAMMING LANGUAGE

ICT113 Mini Project Report

1.0 Introduction

This project presents a Level 2 Quiz Management System developed using the C programming language. The system simulates a basic Learning Management System (LMS) that allows multiple students to register, log in, attempt quizzes, and view results.

The system is designed using structured programming principles and file handling techniques in C. It supports multiple users and stores data persistently using binary files, making it a simple database-like application.

The project also includes an admin functionality that allows the addition of quiz questions, as well as a leaderboard system for displaying student performance.

2.0 Objectives of the Project

The main objectives of this project are:

To develop a multi-user quiz system using C programming.
To implement file handling for persistent data storage.
To simulate a basic login authentication system.
To design a quiz engine with automatic scoring.
To introduce an admin panel for question management.
To display student performance using a leaderboard.

3.0 System Description

The system is a menu-driven application that manages students and quiz questions using two binary files:

students.dat
questions.dat

The system allows:

Student registration
Student login authentication
Quiz participation
Score calculation and storage
Admin question entry
Leaderboard display

4.0 System Features

4.1 Student Registration

Students are able to create an account by entering their name and password. The data is stored in a binary file (students.dat).

4.2 Login System

The system validates student credentials by comparing input data with stored records in the file. Only registered students can access the quiz.

4.3 Quiz Engine

After successful login, students are presented with multiple-choice questions loaded from questions.dat. Each correct answer contributes to the final score.

4.4 Admin Question Management

An admin feature allows new quiz questions to be added to the system. Each question contains:

Question text
Four multiple-choice options
Correct answer indicator

4.5 Score Update System

After completing the quiz, the student’s score is updated in the student database using a temporary file method to ensure safe data modification.

4.6 Leaderboard

The leaderboard displays all registered students along with their quiz scores, allowing comparison of performance.

5.0 System Design and Methodology

The system is built using structured programming in C. It uses two main structures:

Student Structure:
struct Student {
    char name[50];
    char password[50];
    int score;
};

Question Structure:
struct Question {
    char question[100];
    char A[50];
    char B[50];
    char C[50];
    char D[50];
    char correct;
};

5.1 File Handling Approach

The system uses binary file storage:

students.dat → stores student login data and scores
questions.dat → stores quiz questions

File operations used include:

fopen() → open file
fwrite() → write data
fread() → read data
fclose() → close file

A temporary file is used when updating student scores to maintain data integrity.

6.0 Algorithm Design

Quiz Flow Algorithm:
Start system
Display menu
Student logs in
Load quiz questions from file
Display each question
Accept user answer
Compare with correct answer
Calculate total score
Store updated score in file
Display results

7.0 System Advantages
Supports multiple students (multi-user system)
Uses persistent file storage
Simple authentication system
Automatic score calculation
Admin-controlled question system
Leaderboard for performance tracking

8.0 Limitations
Console-based interface only
No encryption for passwords
No random question shuffling (basic version)
No time limit per question
Limited search and analytics features

